---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SignupForm from '../components/SignupForm.astro';
import FreshnessStrip from '../components/viz/FreshnessStrip.astro';
import { loadSiteData, formatUsd } from '../lib/data';
import { SITE_URL } from '../consts';

const data = await loadSiteData();

const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_URL}/methodology/`;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Methodology', url: canonicalUrl },
];

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'What government procurement data sources does NSN Explorer use?',
    answer:
      'Three: DLA DIBBS for solicitations and awards, SAM.gov for contract notices and USAspending for contract actions. All three are public and free.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is any figure estimated?',
    answer:
      'No. Every price, quantity and total is copied from a government record and links to it. The site build rejects any price point without a source URL.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How often is the data refreshed?',
    answer:
      'The site is rebuilt every day from the source systems, and open solicitations are re-crawled more often. Records may still lag the underlying events.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does a price spike mean?',
    answer:
      "A purchase is flagged when its unit price is at least three times the NSN's trailing median. It is a mechanical threshold to prompt a closer look, not a finding.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology: DIBBS, SAM.gov and USAspending Data"
  description="How NSN Explorer builds parts prices and open solicitations from DIBBS, SAM.gov and USAspending records, what each figure means, and how to check any of them."
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  faq={faq}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
  <h1>Government procurement data sources and methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">Methodology</p>
  <p class="lede">
    This page lists the government procurement data sources behind every figure on NSN Explorer
    and explains how each one is used. Prices, solicitations and contract totals come from three
    public systems: DLA's DIBBS bid board, SAM.gov contract notices and USAspending contract
    actions. The index is rebuilt every day, nothing is estimated or modeled, and every number
    links to the record it came from so a supplier, buyer or reporter can check it. The site is
    free.
  </p>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>How current the data is</h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      Each source is checked once a day; the bar shows the age of its newest record against how
      often that source normally updates.
    </p>
    <FreshnessStrip />
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>Unit prices — <code class="mono">pub.price_points</code></h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      Every dot on a price chart and every row in a purchase table is one government purchase at
      one unit price, sourced from either a DIBBS award record or a USAspending award record.
      Each price point stores the underlying source URL, and the build refuses to publish a price
      point without one — that check runs in the data-load step of the site build, not just as a
      database constraint.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>Contract action totals — <code class="mono">pub.contract_actions</code></h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      USAspending publishes agency-wide contract actions with obligation totals — the amount
      obligated on a contract action, not a per-unit price. We show these on NSN pages, clearly
      labeled as "contract action totals, not unit prices," and they never appear on a price
      chart, which is unit-price data only.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>AMSC / AMC — <code class="mono">pub.amsc_observations</code></h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      The Acquisition Method Code (AMC) records whether a part is coded as suitable for
      competitive acquisition (AMC 1 or 2) or to be bought from the actual manufacturer or a
      sole-source contractor (AMC 3, 4 or 5). The suffix code (AMSC) records the reason, such as
      a complete government data package with rights (G) or a need for engineering source
      approval (C). The definitions are those in DFARS PGI 217.7506. We show the most recent
      value on record with its date and source; the main source is DLA's public PUB LOG file.
      The codes describe the item, not any one solicitation.
      See <a href="/guides/amc-amsc-codes/">the guide to AMC and AMSC codes</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>DIBBS data explained</h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      DIBBS is the DLA Internet Bid Board System, where the Defense Logistics Agency posts requests
      for quote and records awards. NSN Explorer reads each open solicitation's NSN, item name,
      quantity, return-by date and set-aside or competition code, and each award's unit price,
      quantity and winning supplier. Solicitations are kept after they close so the demand record
      grows over time. Every DIBBS row on the site links to its record at dibbs.bsm.dla.mil.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>Price spikes and "overcharges"</h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      A purchase is flagged as a spike when its unit price is at least 3× the NSN's trailing
      median price at that point in time. On category pages, a row is highlighted when the most
      recent purchase is at least 3× the NSN's historical median. This is a mechanical threshold,
      not an accusation — it's a starting point for a supplier sanity-checking a quote, or for a
      buyer's, researcher's, or reporter's own follow-up questions, and every flagged purchase
      links to its source record.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>Coverage</h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      We currently fully index {data.deepFscs.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} Federal Supply
      Class{data.deepFscs.length === 1 ? '' : 'es'} ("deep" categories) covering{' '}
      {data.stats.totalNsns.toLocaleString('en-US')} NSNs,{' '}
      {data.stats.totalPurchases.toLocaleString('en-US')} priced purchases, and{' '}
      {data.stats.totalActions.toLocaleString('en-US')} contract actions totaling{' '}
      {formatUsd(data.stats.totalDollars + data.stats.totalObligated)} in indexed spend.
      Government-wide long-tail coverage (millions of NSNs) is on the roadmap; see
      <a href="/browse/">/browse/</a> for the current list.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section id="downloads">
    <h2>Downloads</h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      These CSV files are free to download. They hold the site's own totals and USAspending
      records, and they are rebuilt with the site every day.
    </p>
    <ul class="gloss">
      <li><a href="/data/classes.csv">classes.csv</a>: one row per federal supply class in the catalog, with its group, NSNs tracked, open solicitations, solicitations closing within 7 days, and the count and dollar total of priced purchases.</li>
      <li><a href="/data/groups.csv">groups.csv</a>: one row per federal supply group, with its number of classes, NSNs tracked and open solicitations.</li>
      <li><a href="/data/agencies.csv">agencies.csv</a>: one row per agency, with its indexed purchase count and dollar total.</li>
      <li>contract-actions-&lt;fsc&gt;.csv, such as <code>contract-actions-5330.csv</code>: the USAspending contract actions recorded against one class, with date, award number, PSC, NAICS, description, obligation, agency, recipient and a link to the USAspending record. Each class page links to its file when one exists.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="gloss">
      The <code>as_of</code> column in the first three files is the date of the site build that wrote
      the file. It is the same on every row and changes when the site is rebuilt. Contract-action
      files carry each record's own action date instead. Text that begins with =, +, - or @ is
      written with a leading apostrophe so spreadsheets read it as text. Row-level solicitation
      and award files are not offered yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="faq">
    <h2 class="section-title">Methodology questions</h2>
    {faq.map((f) => (
      <>
        <h3>{f.question}</h3>
        <p class="gloss">{f.answer}</p>
      </>
    ))}
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <SignupForm
    hook="overpay_digest"
    title="A monthly market email is coming"
    description="Leave your email and we'll tell you when the monthly summary of federal parts demand and prices starts. No emails until then."
    cta="Join the list"
  />
</BaseLayout>
